@@ -0,0 +1,52 @@
+---
+type: adr
+status: stable
+entities: [Post]
+related:
+  - app/Http/Controllers/PostController.ts
+  - app/Http/Resources/PostResource.ts
+  - app/Http/Validators/PostValidator.ts
+  - db/schema.ts
+generated: { by: "claude-code/opus-5", at: 2026-08-18T04:55:01.730Z }
+---
+
+# Posts are drafts until published
+
+## Context
+
+Every post used to be public the moment it was saved, so an editor could
+not write across several sessions without the half-finished text being
+readable by anyone who guessed its URL. Editors asked for a plain
+draft/published switch rather than a workflow with review states.
+
+## Decision
+
+`posts.published` is a boolean column that **defaults to false**: a post
+is a draft unless its author ticks the "Published" checkbox on the create
+or edit form. The flag is mass-assignable (`Post.fillable`) and travels
+to and from the pages as `published` on every post payload.
+
+Visibility is enforced in `PostController`, not in `PostPolicy`, because
+it filters collections as well as gating a single record:
+
+- `index` and `search` add one grouping clause — `published = true OR
+  author_id = <viewer>` — so a signed-in author still sees their own
+  drafts while everyone else sees only published posts. It is a
+  `where(callback)` group so the search's keyword ORs cannot swallow it.
+- `show` answers **404** for a draft the viewer did not write, rather
+  than 403 — a 403 would confirm that a draft exists at that id.
+- `edit`/`update`/`destroy` are unchanged: `PostPolicy` already limits
+  them to the author, which is exactly the access a draft needs.
+
+The migration backfills existing rows to `published = true`, so blogs
+(and the demo seeder's posts) that predate the flag do not go dark.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Draft visibility now depends on the viewer, so any new endpoint that
+lists or serves posts must apply the same clause — a post fetched by id
+without the `show` check would leak a draft. Publishing state is a single
+boolean: scheduled publishing or review states would need a further
+decision. Because unauthenticated list and search queries filter on
+`published`, an index on that column is the first thing to add if the
+posts table grows large.
